#*: Expand the series <math>\frac{1}{1-x}-\frac{x}{(1-x)^3}+\frac{1 \cdot 3}{2!}\frac{x^2}{(1-x)^3}-\frac{1 \cdot 3 \cdot 5}{3!}\frac{x^3}{(1-x)^7}+\cdots</math> powers of x, '''shewing''' that the coefficient of <math>x^n</math> is <math>S_{n}=1+\sum_{1}^{n}(-1)^r\frac{(n+r)!}{(r!)^2(n-r)!}\frac{1}{2^r}</math>.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|year_published=1967|author={{w|John Bagnell Bury}}|by={{w|Carl Heinrich Becker}}|title=The Cambridge Medieval History|chapter=The Expansion of the Saracens—The East; Historical aspect of Islam|section=The Rise of the Saracens and the foundation of the Western Empire|publisher=The Macmillan Company|location=New York|editor={{w|Henry Melvill Gwatkin}}; {{w|James Pounder Whitney}}|volume=2|page=330|passage=Within the Christian sphere this current '''shews''' itself more especially in the territories of the Greek and Aramaic languages, and the difference between the Greek and Latin Churches is mainly that between Asia and Europe.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1921|translator=C. K. Moncrieff|author=Marcel Proust|title=Swann's Way|page=1
|passage=I would ask myself what o'clock it could be; I could hear the whistling of trains, which, now nearer and now farther off, punctuating the distance like the note of a bird in a forest, '''shewed''' me in perspective the deserted countryside through which a traveller would be hurrying towards the nearest station: the path that he followed being fixed forever in his memory but the general excitement due to being in a strange place, to farewells exchanged beneath an unfamiliar lamp which echoed still in his ears amid the silence of the night; and to the delightful prospect of being once again at home.}}

